    "body": "Although I appreciate the new algorithmicness of the argument processing, I am not sure of the value of the model. It seems like it's attempting to make something more rigorous but not really succeeding.\r\n\r\nWhat is the purpose of the store at all? It isn't normatively referenced anywhere in the spec except to add/remove from it. We're planning on keeping the existing \"This section does not define how the pages registered by this method are used.\" paragraph, I assume---in which case I don't see the store ever being useful. So I'd prefer the existing specification's \"you can do whatever you like,\" as that's at least honest about being vague and allowing implementation freedom.\r\n\r\nAnd, as commented inline, I don't think a parallel queue is useful here, as there are no steps that need to be performed atomically (e.g. you never check if the handler already exists in the store or anything like that).\r\n\r\nWe also seem to have lost this:\r\n\r\n> User agents should keep track of which sites have registered handlers (even if the user has declined such registrations) so that the user is not repeatedly prompted with the same request.",
